Date: Tue, 18 Aug 2026 12:49:54 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 070/154] feat(sidebar): reveal a hidden pane from the window
 edge, and take a tab back
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Two gestures that were built and unreachable.

THE EDGE REVEAL. AtriumSidebarOverlayWidget needs a second sidebar NSView
from a Swift entry point that no longer exists, so nothing had been able to
construct it and the reveal was dead rather than broken. The replacement
opens the pane the window ALREADY has, through the same SetSidebarWidth path
the toggle uses - so there is no second view to keep in sync and whichever
rail pane was showing comes back untouched.

The zone has two widths. Hidden, it is a thin strip, narrow enough that
reading a page does not arm it. Open by hover, it covers the pane, or the
pointer moving onto the sidebar it just revealed would read as leaving and
the pane would flicker shut under the cursor. The zone never takes an event:
hitTest returns nil, and a tracking area is geometric, so clicks pass to the
page beneath while enter and exit still report.

A hover reveal is not a dock. The toggle on a hover-revealed pane KEEPS it,
rather than hiding it, so the chord's meaning does not depend on how the pane
came to be open - something the user cannot see.

TAKING A TAB BACK. BrowserController::ActivateTab has done the cross-window
move, the desaturated freeze of the losing pane and the becomes-active focus
since M2c, but its only caller was the retired Swift ABI, so it sat private
with no caller at all. Made public, and ActivateSidebarTab now falls through
to it instead of logging the row as stale.

Signed-off-by: János Mikó <693558+janosmiko@users.noreply.github.com>
